\f2 \cf2 Tant\'f4t l'on invoque des facteurs exog\'e8nes, li\'e9s \'e0 la guerre et \'e0 la machine de guerre ; tant\'f4t des facteurs endog\'e8nes, qui feraient na\'eetre la propri\'e9t\'e9 priv\'e9e, la monnaie, etc. ; tant\'f4t enfin des facteurs sp\'e9cifiques qui d\'e9termineraient la formation de \'ab fonctions publiques \'bb . On trouve les trois th\'e8ses chez Engels, suivant une conception de la diversit\'e9 des voies de la Domination. Mais elles supposent ce qui est en question. La guerre ne produit d'Etat que si l'une des deux parties au moins est un Etat pr\'e9alable ; et l'organisation de guerre n'est facteur d'Etat que si elle lui appartient. Ou bien l'Etat ne comporte pas de machine de guerre (il a des policiers et des ge\'f4liers avant d'avoir des soldats), ou bien il en comporte, mais sous forme d'institution militaire ou de fonction publique 8. De m\'eame la propri\'e9t\'e9 prh\'e9e suppose une propri\'e9t\'e9 publique d'Etat, elle coule \'e0 travers ses mailles ; et la monnaie suppose l'imp\'f4t. Et l'on voit plus mal encore comment des fonctions publiques pourraient pr\'e9exister \'e0 l'Etat qu'elles impliquent. On est toujours renvoy\'e9 \'e0 un Etat qui na\'eet adulte et qui surgit d'un coup, Urstaat inconditionn\'e9.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 At times, exogenous factors, tied to war and the war machine, are invoked; at times endogenous factors, thought to engender private property, money, etc.; and at times specific factors, thought to determine the formation of "public functions." All three of these theses are found in Engels, in relation to a conception of the diversity of the roads to Domination. But they beg the question. War produces the State only if at least one of the two parts is a preexistent State; and the organization of war is a State factor only if that organization is a part of the State. Either the State has no war machine (and has policemen and jailers before having soldiers), or else it has one, but in the form of a military institution or public function.8 Similarly, private property presupposes State public property, it slips through its net; and money presupposes taxation. It is even more difficult to see how public functions could have existed before the State they imply. We are always brought back to the idea of a State that comes into the world fully formed and rises up in a single stroke, the unconditioned Urstaat.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Sometimes one 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 invoque
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [invokes \'97 the legal summons again (\'b66, authors invoqu\'e9s): factors called as authorities, and found insufficient]\cf0 \strokec2  exogenous factors, tied to war and the war machine; at times endogenous factors, which 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 feraient na\'eetre
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [would give birth to \'97 faire na\'eetre, the causative-of-birth idiom; conditional kept: alleged parentage]\cf0 \strokec2  private property, money, etc.; at times finally specific factors which 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 d\'e9termineraient
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [would determine \'97 conditional of the reported claim, second of the pair]\cf0 \strokec2  the formation of \'ab public functions \'bb. One finds the three theses in Engels, following a conception of the diversity of the 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 voies de la Domination
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [ways of Domination \'97 voie: the road register, also the legal voie (voie de droit, legal avenue); capital D in the French]\cf0 \strokec2 . But they 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 supposent
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [suppose \'97 the supposer chain of \'b66, now the refutation-engine: each thesis supposes what it must explain]\cf0 \strokec2  what is in question. War produces a State only if one of the two 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 parties
