\f2 \cf2 Nous avons vu notamment comment Pierre Clastres avait tent\'e9 de briser le cadre \'e9volutionniste, en fonction de deux th\'e8ses : 1 ) les soci\'e9t\'e9s dites primitives n'\'e9taient pas des soci\'e9t\'e9s sans Etat, au sens o\'f9 elles n'auraient pas atteint un certain stade, mais des soci\'e9t\'e9s contre-Etat, organisant des m\'e9canismes qui conjuraient la forme-Etat, qui en rendaient la cristallisation impossible ; 2) quand l'Etat surgit, c'est sous forme d'une coupure irr\'e9ductible, puisqu'il n'est pas la cons\'e9quence d'un d\'e9veloppement progressif des forces productives (m\'eame la \'ab r\'e9volution n\'e9olithique \'bb ne peut pas se d\'e9finir en fonction d'une infrastructure \'e9conomique 12). Toutefois, on ne rompt pas avec l'\'e9volutionnisme en tra\'e7ant une coupure pour elle-m\'eame : Clastres, dans l'\'e9tat dernier de son travail, maintenait la pr\'e9existence et l'autarcie des soci\'e9t\'e9s contre-Etat, et attribuait leur m\'e9canisme \'e0 un pressentiment trop myst\'e9rieux de ce qu'elles conjuraient et qui n'existait pas encore. Plus g\'e9n\'e9ralement, on s'\'e9tonne de la bizarre indiff\'e9rence que l'ethnologie manifeste encore \'e0 l'\'e9gard de l 'arch\'e9ologie. On dirait que les ethnologues, enferm\'e9s dans leurs territoires respectifs, veulent bien les comparer entre eux de mani\'e8re abstraite, ou structurale, \'e0 la rigueur, mais refusent de les confronter aux territoires arch\'e9ologiques qui en compromettraient l'autarcie. Ils tirent des photos de leurs primitifs, mais r\'e9cusent d'avance la coexistence et la superposition des deux cartes, ethnographique et arch\'e9ologique. \'c7atal H\'fcy\'fck aurait eu pourtant une zone d'influence de trois mille kilom\'e8tres ; et comment laisser dans le vague le probl\'e8me toujours pos\'e9 du rapport de coexistence entre les soci\'e9t\'e9s primitives et les empires, m\'eame du n\'e9olithique ?
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 We have seen in particular how Pierre Clastres tried to shatter the evolutionist framework by means of the following two theses: ( 1 ) societies termed primitive are not societies without a State, in the sense that they failed to reach a certain stage, but are counter-State societies organizing mechanisms that ward off the State-f orm, which make its crystallization impossible; (2) when the State arises, it is in the form of an irreducible break, since it is not the result of a progressive development of the forces of production (even the "Neolithic revolution" cannot be defined in terms of an economic infrastructure)Y However, one does not depart from evolutionism by establishing a clean break. In the final state of his work, Clastres maintained the preexistence and autarky of counter-State societies, and attributed their workings to an overmysterious presentiment of what they warded off and did not yet exist. More generally, one marvels at the bizarre indifference that ethnology manif ests for archaeology. It seems as though ethnologists, fenced of f in their respective territories, are willing to compare their territories in an abstract, or structural, way, if it comes to that, but ref use to set them against archaeological territories that would compromise their autarky. They take snapshots oftheir primitives but rule out in advance the coexistence and superposition of the two maps, the ethnographical and the archaeological. Catal Htiytik, however, would have had a zone of influence extending two thousand miles; how can the everrecurring problem of the relation of coexistence between primitive societies and empires, even those of Paleolithic times, be left unattended to?
\f1 \cell 
