\f2 \cf2 C'est un ph\'e9nom\'e8ne de trans-consistance, c'est un r\'e9seau, parce qu'elle est fondamentalement en rapport avec d'autres villes. Elle repr\'e9sente un seuil de d\'e9territorialisation, car il faut que le mat\'e9riau quelconque soit suffisamment d\'e9territorialis\'e9 pour entrer dans le r\'e9seau, se soumettre \'e0 la polarisation, suivre le circuit de recodage urbain et routier. Le maximum de d\'e9territorialisation appara\'eet dans la tendance des villes commerciales et maritimes \'e0 se s\'e9parer de l'arri\'e8re-pays, de la campagne (Ath\'e8nes, Carthage, Venise . . . ). On a souvent insist\'e9 sur le caract\'e8re commercial de la ville, mais le commerce y est aussi bien spirituel, comme dans un r\'e9seau de monast\'e8res ou de cit\'e9s-temples. Les villes sont des points-circuits de toute nature, qui font contrepoint sur les lignes horizontales ; elles op\'e8rent une int\'e9gration compl\'e8te, mais locale, et de ville en ville. Chacune constitue un pouvoir central, mais de polarisation ou de milieu, de coordination forc\'e9e. D'o\'f9 la pr\'e9tention \'e9galitaire de ce pouvoir, quelle que soit la forme qu'il prenne, tyrannique, d\'e9mocratique, oligarchique, aristocratique . . . L e pouvoir d e ville invente l'id\'e9e d e magistrature, tr\'e8s diff\'e9rente du fonctionnariat d'Etat 16. Mais qui dira o\'f9 est la plus grande violence civile ? En effet, l'Etat proc\'e8de autrement : c'est un ph\'e9nom\'e8ne d'intra-consistance. Il fait r\'e9sonner ensemble des points, qui ne sont pas forc\'e9ment d\'e9j\'e0 des villes-p\'f4les, mais des points d'ordre tr\'e8s divers, particularit\'e9s g\'e9ographiques, ethniques, linguistiques, morales, \'e9conomiques, technologiques . . . Il fait r\'e9sonner la ville avec la campagne. Il op\'e8re par stratification, c'est-\'e0-dire forme un ensemble vertical et hi\'e9rarchis\'e9 qui traverse les lignes horizon16.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 It is a phenomenon of transconsistency, a network, because it is fundamentally in contact with other towns. It represents a threshold of deterritorialization, because whatever the material involved, it must be deterritorialized enough to enter the network, to submit to the polarization, to follow the circuit of urban and road recoding. The maximum deterritorialization appears in the tendency of maritime and commercial towns to separate off from the backcountry, from the countryside (Athens, Carthage, Venice). The commercial character of the town has often been emphasized, but the commerce in question is also spiritual, as in a network of monasteries or temple-cities. Towns are circuit-points of every kind, which enter into counterpoint along horizontal lines; they effect a complete but local, town-by-town, integration. Each one constitutes a central power, but it is a power of polarization or of the middle (milieu), off orced coordination. That is why this kind of power has egalitarian pretensions, regardless of the form it takes: tyrannical, democratic, oligarchic, aristocratic. Town power invents the idea of the magis- 7000 B.c.: APPARA TUS OF CAPTURE 0 433 trature, which is very different from the State civil-service sector (f onctionnariat).16 Who can say where the greatest civil violence resides?
\f1 \

\f2 The State indeed proceeds otherwise: it is a phenomenon of intraconsistency. It makes points resonate together, points that are not necessarily already town-poles but very diverse points of order, geographic, ethnic, linguistic, moral, economic, technological particularities. It makes the town resonate with the countryside. It operates by stratification; in other words, it forms a vertical, hierarchized aggregate that spans the horizontal lines in a dimension of depth.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

