\f2 \cf2 \'b634 fr.547 umn.439
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 On voit que l'utilit\'e9 marginale (aff\'e9rente aux derniers objets r\'e9ceptibles des deux c\'f4t\'e9s ) n'est nullement relative \'e0 un stock abstraitement suppos\'e9, mais \'e0 l'agencement respectif des deux groupes. Pareto allait dans cette direction quand il parlait d' \'ab oph\'e9limit\'e9 \'bb plut\'f4t que d'utilit\'e9 marginale. Il s'agit d'une d\'e9sirabilit\'e9 comme composante d'agencement : chaque groupe d\'e9sire suivant la valeur du dernier objet r\'e9ceptible, au-del\'e0 duquel il serait forc\'e9 de changer d'agencement. Et tout agencement a pr\'e9cis\'e9ment deux faces, machination de corps ou d'objets, \'e9nonciation de groupe. L'\'e9valuation du dernier est l'\'e9nonciation collective \'e0 laquelle correspond toute la s\'e9rie des objets, c'est-\'e0-dire un cycle ou un exercice d'agencement. Les groupes primitifs \'e9changistes apparaissent ainsi comme des groupes s\'e9riels. C'est un r\'e9gime sp\'e9cial, m\'eame du point de vue de la violence. Car m\'eame la violence peut \'eatre soumise \'e0 un traitement rituel marginal, c'est-\'e0-dire \'e0 une \'e9valuation de la \'ab derni\'e8re violence \'bb comme impr\'e9gnant toute la s\'e9rie des coups ( au-del\'e0 commencerait un autre r\'e9gime de violence) . Nous d\'e9finissions pr\'e9c\'e9demment les soci\'e9t\'e9s primitives par l'existence de m\'e9canismes d'anticipation-conjuration. Nous voyons mieux comment ces m\'e9canismes se constituent et se distribuent : c'est l'\'e9valuation du dernier comme limite qui constitue une anticipation, laquelle conjure en m\'eame temps le dernier comme seuil ou comme ultime (nouvel agencement). Le seuil est \'ab apr\'e8s \'bb la limite, \'ab apr\'e8s \'bb les derniers objets r\'e9ceptibles : il marque le moment o\'f9 l'\'e9change apparent ne pr\'e9sente plus d'int\'e9r\'eat. Or nous croyons que le stock commence pr\'e9cis\'e9ment \'e0 ce moment ; auparavant, il peut y avoir des greniers d'\'e9change, des greniers \'e0 \'e9change, mais pas de stock \'e0 proprement parler.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 It can be seen that marginal utility (pertaining to the last objects receivable on both sides) is relative not to an abstractly posited stock but to the respective assemblages of the two groups. Pareto was moving in this direction when he spoke of "ophelimity" rather than of marginal utility. 26 The issue is one of desirability as an assemblage component: every group desires according to the value of the last receivable object beyond which it would be obliged to change assemblage. And every assemblage has two sides, the machining of bodies or objects, and group enunciation. The evaluation of the last is the collective enunciation to which the entire series of objects corresponds; in other words, it is an assemblage cycle or operation period. Exchangist primitive groups thus appear to be serial groups. Theirs is a special regime, even with respect to violence. For even violence can be submitted to a marginal ritual treatment, that is, to an evaluation of the "last violence" insof ar as it impregnates the entire series of blows (beyond which another regime of violence would begin). We previously defined primitive societies by the existence of anticipation-prevention mechanisms. Now we can see more clearly how these mechanisms are constituted and distributed: it is the evaluation of the last as limit that constitutes an anticipation and simultaneously wards off the last as threshold or ultimate (a new assemblage).
\f1 \

\f2 The threshold comes "after" the limit, "af ter" the last receivable objects: it marks the moment when the apparent exchange is no longer of interest. We believe that it is precisely at this moment that stockpiling begins; beforehand, there may be exchange granaries, granaries specifically for exchange purposes, but there is no stock in the strict sense.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

