\f6 \cf2 La guerre, du moins rapport\'e9e \'e0 la machine de guerre, est un autre r\'e9gime, parce qu'elle implique la mobilisation et l'autonomisation d'une violence dirig\'e9e d'abord et en principe contre l'appareil d'Etat ( la machine de guerre en ce sens est l'invention d'une organisation nomade originale qui se retourne contre l'Etat). Le crime est encore diff\'e9rent, parce que c'est une violence d'ill\'e9galit\'e9, qui consiste \'e0 s 'emparer de quelque chose \'e0 quoi l'on n'a pas \'ab droit \'bb, de capturer quelque chose qu'on n'a pas le \'ab droit \'bb de capturer. Mais, justement, la police d\}Etat ou violence de droit est encore diff\'e9rente, puisqu'elle consiste \'e0 capturer, tout en constituant un droit de capture. C'est une violence structurelle, incorpor\'e9e, qui s'oppose \'e0 toutes les violences directes. On a souvent d\'e9fini l'Etat par un \'ab monopole de la violence \'bb, mais cette d\'e9finition renvoie \'e0 une autre, qui d\'e9termine l'Etat comme \'ab \'e9tat du Droit \'bb (Rechtsstaat). Le surcodage d'Etat, c'est pr\'e9cis\'e9ment cette violence structurelle qui d\'e9finit le droit, violence \'ab polici\'e8re \'bb et non guerri\'e8re. Il y a violence de droit chaque fois que la violence contribue \'e0 cr\'e9er ce sur quoi elle s'exerce, ou, comme dit Marx, chaque fois gue la capture contribue \'e0 cr\'e9er ce qu'elle capture. C'est tr\'e8s diff\'e9rent de la violence de crime. C'est pourquoi aussi, \'e0 l\}inverse de la violence primitive, la violence de droit ou d'Etat semble toujours se pr\'e9supposer, puisqu'elle pr\'e9existe \'e0 son propre exercice : l'Etat peut alors dire que la violence est \'ab originelle \'bb, simple ph\'e9nom\'e8ne de nature, et qu'il n'en est pas responsable, lui qui n'exerce la 35. Jean Robert montre bien, en ce sens, que l'accumulation originelle implique la construction violente d'un espace homog\'e9n\'e9is\'e9, \'ab colonis\'e9 \'bb.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6 \cf2 War, at least when linked to the war machine, is another regime, because it implies the mobilization and autonomization of a violence directed first and essentially against the State apparatus (the war machine is in this sense the invention of a primary nomadic organization that turns against the State). Crime is something else, because it is a violence of illegality that consists in taking possession of something to which one has no "right," in capturing something one does not have a "right" to capture. But State policing or law ful violence is something else again, because it consists in capturing while simultaneously constituting a right to capture. It is an incorporated, structural violence distinct from every kind of direct violence. The State has often been defined by a "monopoly of violence," but this definition leads back to another definition that describes the State as a "state of Law" (Rechtsstaat). State overcoding is precisely this structural violence that defines the law, "police" violence and not the violence of war. There is lawf ul violence wherever violence contributes to the creation of that which it is used against, or as Marx says, wherever capture contributes to the creation of that which it captures. This is very diff erent from criminal violence. It is also why, in contradistinction to primitive violence, State or lawful violence always seems to presuppose itself, for it preexists its own use: the State can in this way say that violence is "primal," that it is simply a natural phenomenon the responsibility for which does not lie with the State, which uses violence only against the violent, against "criminals" -against primitives, against nomads-in order that peace may reign.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

