\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6 \cf2 On n'arrive m\'eame pas \'e0 pr\'e9voir l'augmentation d'une masse mon\'e9taire. De m\'eame, les Etats sont affect\'e9s par toutes sortes de coefficients d'incertitude et d'impr\'e9vision. Galbraith, Fran\'e7ois Ch\'e2telet, d\'e9gagent le concept d'erreurs d\'e9cisives et constantes, qui font la gloire des hommes d'Etat non moins que leurs rares \'e9valuations r\'e9ussies. Or, c'est une raison de plus pour rapprocher politique et axiomatique. Car une axiomatique en science n'est nullement une puissance transcendante, autonome et d\'e9cisoire, qui s'opposerait \'e0 l'exp\'e9rimentation et \'e0 l'intuition. D'une part, elle a des t\'e2tonnements, des exp\'e9rimentations, des modes d'intuition qui lui sont propres. Les axiomes \'e9tant ind\'e9pendants les uns des autres, peut-on ajouter des axiomes, et jusqu'\'e0 quel point ( syst\'e8me satur\'e9 ) ? Retirer des axiomes, et jusqu'\'e0 quel point ( syst\'e8me \'ab affaibli \'bb ) ? D'autre part, il appartient \'e0 l'axiomatique de se heurter \'e0 des propositions dites ind\'e9cidables, ou d'affronter des puissances n\'e9cessairement sup\'e9rieures qu'elle ne peut pas ma\'eetriser 54. Enfin, l'axiomatique ne constitue pas une pointe de la science, mais beaucoup plus un point d'arr\'eat, une remise en ordre, qui emp\'eache les flux s\'e9miotiques d\'e9cod\'e9s, math\'e9matiques et physiques, de fuir de toutes parts. Les grands axiomaticiens sont des hommes d'Etat de la science, qui colmatent les lignes de fuite si fr\'e9quentes en math\'e9matiques, qui pr\'e9tendent imposer un nouveau nexum, m\'eame provisoire, et font une politique officielle de la science.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6 \cf2 No one is even capable of predicting the growth in the money supply. Similarly, the States are aff ected by all kinds of coef ficients of uncertainty and unpredictability. John Kenneth Galbraith and Fran90is CMtelet have formulated the concept of constant and decisi ve errors, which make the glory of men of State no less than their rare successf ul evaluations. But that is just one more reason to make a connection between politics and axiomatics. For in science an axiomatic is not at all a transcendent, autonomous, and decision-making power opposed to experimentation and intuition. On the one hand, it has its own gropings in the dark, experimentations, modes of intuition. Axioms being independent of each other, can they be added, and up to what point (a saturated system)? Can they be withdrawn (a "weakened" system)? On the other hand, it is of the nature of ax ioma tics to come up against so-called undecidable propositions, to conf ront necessarily higher powers that it cannot master.60 Finally, axiomatics does not constitute the cutting edge of science; it is much more a stopping point, a reordering that prevents decoded semiotic flows in physics and mathematics from escaping in all directions. The great axiomaticians are the men of State of science, who seal off the lines of flight that are so frequent in mathematics, who would impose a new nexum, if only a temporary one, and who lay down the of ficial policies of science.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 One does not even manage to foresee the augmentation of a 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 masse mon\'e9taire
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2 . Likewise, the States are affected by all sorts of 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 coefficients
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  of uncertainty and imprevision \cf3 \strokec3 [coefficients \'97 the equation's multipliers: uncertainty quantified into the State's own mathematics]\cf0 \strokec2 . Galbraith, Fran\'e7ois Ch\'e2telet, 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 d\'e9gagent
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  the concept of 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 erreurs d\'e9cisives et constantes, qui font la gloire des hommes d'\'c9tat
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [decisive and constant errors, which make the glory of the men of State \'97 the \'b61 hommes d'\'c9tat: the Borgne and Manchot's heirs, glorified now by their errors]\cf0 \strokec2  no less than their rare successful 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 \'e9valuations
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [evaluations \'97 the \'b630 marginal machinery: statecraft as evaluation, mostly failed]\cf0 \strokec2 . 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 Or
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2 , it is one more reason to bring together politics and axiomatique. For an axiomatic in science is in no way a transcendent, autonomous and 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 d\'e9cisoire
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  power, 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 qui s'opposerait \'e0
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  experimentation and intuition \cf3 \strokec3 [which would oppose itself to \'97 conditional + reflexive: the caricature voiced in its mood]\cf0 \strokec2 . On the one hand, it has 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 t\'e2tonnements
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2 , experimentations, modes of intuition 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 qui lui sont propres
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2 . The axioms being independent of one another, 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 peut-on ajouter des axiomes, et jusqu'\'e0 quel point
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  (saturated system)? 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 Retirer des axiomes, et jusqu'\'e0 quel point
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  (\'ab 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 affaibli
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \'bb system)? \cf3 \strokec3 [can one add axioms, and up to what point\'85 withdraw axioms, and up to what point \'97 the jusqu'\'e0 quel point repeated for both operations: addition and subtraction each with its threshold; \'ab weakened \'bb system, the logician's term]\cf0 \strokec2  On the other hand, 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 il appartient \'e0 l'axiomatique de se heurter \'e0
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  propositions 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 dites ind\'e9cidables
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [it belongs to the axiomatic to run up against \'97 appartenir + se heurter: collision as the axiomatic's property; the undecidables of fn. 48/54]\cf0 \strokec2 , or to 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 affronter des puissances n\'e9cessairement sup\'e9rieures qu'elle ne peut pas ma\'eetriser
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [confront necessarily superior powers it cannot master \'97 puissances: the cardinalities again, the continuum outranking every axiom-set]
\f5 \cf0 \strokec2 \uc0\u8309 \u8308 
\f3 . Finally, the axiomatic does not constitute 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 une pointe de la science
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2 , but much more 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 un point d'arr\'eat
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [a point of science\'85 a stopping-point \'97 pointe/point: the spearhead against the halt, one word sharpened then blunted; arr\'eat (\'b614): the arrest that fixes]\cf0 \strokec2 , a 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 remise en ordre
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2 , which prevents the decoded semiotic flows, mathematical and physical, 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 de fuir de toutes parts
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [from fleeing on all sides \'97 fuir: the flight-verb, mathematics leaking]\cf0 \strokec2 . 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 Les grands axiomaticiens sont des hommes d'\'c9tat de la science
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \cf3 \strokec3 [the great axiomaticians are the men of State of science \'97 the \'b61 figure transposed whole: science has its Borgnes and Manchots]\cf0 \strokec2 , who 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 colmatent les lignes de fuite
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  so frequent in mathematics \cf3 \strokec3 [caulk the lines of flight \'97 colmater: to plug a leak, seal a breach: the hydraulic sealing-verb applied to the book's own escape-concept]\cf0 \strokec2 , who 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 pr\'e9tendent imposer un nouveau nexum
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2 , even provisional \cf3 \strokec3 [claim to impose a new nexum \'97 pr\'e9tendre (\'b631): the legal claim; nexum: the \'b69 bond as modern science-policy \'97 the archaic lien in the seminar room]\cf0 \strokec2 , and make an official politics of science.\

\f5 \uc0\u8309 \u8308 
\f3  These are the two great problems of the axiomatic, historically: the encounter with \'ab undecidable \'bb propositions (contradictory 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 \'e9nonc\'e9s
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  are equally undemonstrable); the encounter with 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 puissances
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  of infinite sets that escape by nature axiomatic 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 traitement
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  (\'ab the continuum, for example, cannot be conceived axiomatically in its structural specificity, since every axiomatic one gives of it 
\f4\i\b \strokec2 comportera un mod\'e8le d\'e9nombrable
\f3\i0\b0 \strokec2  \'bb \cf3 \strokec3 [will comprise a denumerable model \'97 the L\'f6wenheim\'96Skolem theorem: every axiomatization of the continuum admits a countable model, the Skolem paradox: the escape of the infinite formalized]\cf0 \strokec2 ; cf. Robert Blanch\'e9, p. 80). \cf3 \strokec3 (French 54 = Minnesota 60)\cf0 \strokec2 \
