\f6 \cf2 59. Braudel montre comment ce centre de gravit\'e9 se constituera au nord de l'Europe, mais \'e0 l'issue de mouvements qui, d\'e8s le IXt' et le xc si\'e8cles, font concourir ou rivaliser les espaces europ\'e9ens du Nord et du Sud (cc probl\'e8me ne se confond pas avec celui de la forme-ville et de la forme-Etat, mais il le recoupe). Cf. \'ab Naissance d'une \'e9conomie-monde \'bb, in Urbi, l, septembre 1979. et Illl'Ille ql l ' i l Y a des Sud ou des tiers mondes int\'e9rieurs au cen t re . I l l'st cla i r aussi que cette d\'e9stabilisation n'est pas accidente l l e , mais l'st une cons\'e9quence ( th\'e9or\'e9matique) des axiomes du capi t a l isllle , ct principalement de l'axiome dit de f\'e9change in\'e9f,al, i nd ispensable \'e0 son fonctionnement.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f6 \cf2 65. Braudel shows how this center of gravity formed in northern Europe, but at the outcome of movements that, starting in the ninth and tenth centuries, put the European spaces of the North and the South in competition or rivalry with one another (this problem is not to be confused with that of the town-form and State-f orm, but does intersect with it). See "Naissance d'une economie-monde," Urbi, no. I (September 1 979), pp. 3-20.<br>and even that there are Souths or Third Worlds inside the center. It is also clear that this destabilization is not accidental but is a (theorematic) consequence of the axioms of capitalism, principally of the axiom called unequal exchange, which is indispensable to capitalism's functioning.
\f1 \cell 
\pard\intbl\itap1\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

